  "summary": "Grotesque spirit-beasts carved from tooth and bone — vengeance objects turned art.",
  "description": "A tupilaq was originally a shaman's made-to-order monster: bones, hide and hair assembled in secret, brought to life by song, and set into the sea to destroy an enemy — with the deadly risk of returning against its maker. When 19th-century East Greenlanders could safely show the concept to outsiders, they carved tupilaq instead from sperm-whale tooth, walrus ivory, narwhal tusk and antler: hunched, snaggle-toothed, many-eyed figures of pure nightmare energy. Collectors from Jean Malaurie onward made the small carvings Greenland's signature export art, and Ammassalik carvers still cut each figure's grimace by hand.",
  "culturalContext": "Tupilaq transformed from secret shaman's weapon into Greenland's signature export art after 19th-century East Greenlanders could safely show the concept; carvers in Ammassalik and Nuuk sustain the market, and the little monsters now carry Kalaallit identity into global galleries and national symbolism.",
